      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02, Revision 2\n- Scheme: A\n- Member state: Czech Republic\n- Issuing authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Signed by: RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\n- Date issued: 2 February 2023\n- Page total: 15\n\nApplicant: ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA, Via F.lli Gracchi, 27; Cinisello Balsamo; 20 099 Italy\nManufacturer: Hemina SPA, Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD; PC: 350 44, Italy\n\nNote: The characteristics tables say \"Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\" — slightly different address, but I'll keep the Manufacturer section as given on page 1.\n\nCertified type:\n- MS2500 / MV110\n- MS2500 / MV145\n- Category: Water meter (R49 is water meters) — the description says \"electromagnetic water meters\". Category could be \"Water meter\" or \"Measuring system\". The schema examples include \"Gas meter\", \"Taximeter\", etc. R49 is \"Water meters\". I'll use \"Water meter\".\n\nModule designation: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\", \"MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery\", \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery\", \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\" — these are module variants. Actually the module designation field lists multiple module configurations. I'll capture these.\n\nRecommendation: R49, edition 2013, scheme A, accuracy classes [1, 2]\n\nTest reports:\n- 0511-ER-V053-22, dated 10 August 2022, 381 pages including annexes 1-5 — role: type_evaluation_report\n- 6015-PT-P5009-22, 21 pages — test_report\n- 8551-PT-E0072-22, 15 pages — test_report\n- 6011-PT-SW012-22, 6 pages — test_report\n- 6015-PT-P5001-22 Revision 1, 104 pages — test_report\n- 6015-PT-P5001-22, 94 pages — test_report\n- 6011-PT-SW009-22, 9 pages — test_report\n- 8551-PT-E0041-21, 29 pages — test_report\n\nDocumentation file: 0511-UL-V053-20\n\nRevision history:\n- Revision 0, 26 May 2022, Issuing certificate\n- Revision 1, 13 September 2022, Adding electronics MV145\n- Revision 2, 2 February 2023, Corrected the battery parameters in the electronics MV145 and corrected adding MV145 for DN>=200 mm\n\nNow the characteristics. This is a complex multi-DN table structure. The models vary by:\n- Converter: MV110 vs MV145\n- Nominal diameter: 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350\n- Q3/Q1 ratio: 250 or 400 (accuracy class 1 corresponds to Q3/Q1=250, class 2 to Q3/Q1=400)\n\nFor each DN, there are two variants: class 1 (Q3/Q1=250) and class 2 (Q3/Q1=400).\n\nData from tables:\n\nDN25: Q1: 0.064 (Q3/Q1=250), 0.040 (Q3/Q1=400); Q2: 0.102, 0.064; Q3: 16.00, 16.00; Q4: 20.00, 20.00; Q3/Q1: 250, 400\nDN32: Q1: 0.100, 0.062; Q2: 0.160, 0.100; Q3: 25.00, 25.00; Q4: 31.25, 31.25\nDN40: Q1: 0.160, 0.100; Q2: 0.256, 0.160; Q3: 40.00, 40.00; Q4: 50.00, 50.00\nDN50: Q1: 0.252, 0.158; Q2: 0.403, 0.252; Q3: 63.0, 63.0; Q4: 73.0, 73.0\n\nWait, DN50 Q4 is 73.0? That's odd — should be 78.75 for Q4/Q3=1.25 with Q3=63. But table says 73.0. I'll record as-is (don't fabricate).\n\nHmm, actually let me re-check: \"Q4 [m3/h]: 73.0 | 73.0\". Yes, as printed. I'll record 73.0.\n\nDN65: Q1: 0.400, 0.250; Q2: 0.640, 0.400; Q3: 100, 100; Q4: 125, 125\nDN80: Q1: 0.640, 0.400; Q2: 1.024, 0.640; Q3: 160, 160; Q4: 200, 200\nDN100: Q1: 1.00, 0.63; Q2: 1.60, 1.00; Q3: 250, 250; Q4: 313, 313\nDN125: Q1: 1.60, 1.00; Q2: 2.56, 1.60; Q3: 400, 400; Q4: 500, 500\nDN150: Q1: 2.52, 1.60; Q2: 4.03, 1.56; Q3: 630, 630; Q4: 780, 780\n\nNote DN150 Q2 for Q3/Q1=400 is 1.56 (printed). As printed — 1.56. Hmm interesting, 1.56 vs 1.6*1.56... Q2/Q1 = 1.6 → 1.60*1.56 = wait Q1=1.60, so Q2 should be 2.56. Printed 1.56. Record as printed.\n\nDN200: Q1: 4.0, 2.5; Q2: 6.4, 4.0; Q3: 1000, 1000; Q4: 1250, 1250\nDN250: Q1: 6.4, 4.0; Q2: 10.2, 6.4; Q3: 1600, 1600; Q4: 2000, 2000\nDN300: Q1: 6.4, 4.0; Q2: 10.2, 6.4; Q3: 1600, 1600; Q4: 2000, 2000\nDN350: Q1: 6.4, 4.0; Q2: 10.2, 6.4; Q3: 1600, 1600; Q4: 2000, 2000\n\nCommon attributes across all DNs:\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Q4/Q3: 1.25\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic water meter\n- MPE_l: ±3% (class 1), ±5% (class 2)\n- MPE_u: ±1% (class1), ±2% (class2) for ≤30°C; ±2% (class1), ±3% (class2) for >30°C\n- Temperature class: per liner\n- Water pressure class: MAP16\n- Pressure loss class: Δp40\n- Reverse flow: designed to measure\n- Environmental class: O (-25 to +55 °C)\n- Electromagnetic environment: E1, E2\n- Mechanical class: B, O\n- Max admissible temperature: PTFE 90°C; Polypropylene/Rilsan/Ebonite 50°C\n- Max admissible pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- Orientation limitation: any\n- Resolution of device for rapid testing: - (none) — omit\n- EUT category: Electromagnetic water meter\n- Case: D\n- Connection type: Flanged\n- Min straight length inlet: 0 mm\n- Min straight length outlet: 0 mm\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\n- Flow conditioner: No\n- Orientation: any\n\nModel-varying attributes:\n- Indicating range [m³]: DN25-40: 99 999; DN50: 99 999 (first table on page 5 shows DN50: \"99 999 | 999 999 | 999 999\" — wait, let me check. Page 5 table covers DN50, 65, 80. Indicating range: colspan 2 \"99 999\", then \"999 999\", then \"999 999\". So DN50: 99 999, DN65: 999 999, DN80: 999 999. Hmm, that's odd but as printed.\n\nWait, page 4's table (DN25, 32, 40) is on page 5 actually. Let me re-check page ordering:\n\nPage 4 table: DN 25, 32, 40. Includes:\n- Indicating range: colspan2 99 999, then 99 999, then 99 999, then empty. So DN25: 99999, DN32: 99999, DN40: 99999.\n- Resolution: 0.000 1 for all three.\n- Length: 200, 200, 200.\n\nPage 5 table (continuation): This is the second half of the DN25/32/40 table? Actually page 5 first table starts with \"Maximum admissible pressure [MPa]: 1.6\" and includes Indicating range 99 999, 99 999, 99 999; Resolution 0.000 1 ×3; Length 200, 200, 200. Hmm wait.\n\nLet me re-read. Page 4 table: Manufacturer, Model number, Nominal diameter 25/32/40, Q1-Q4, Q3/Q1, Q2/Q1, Q4/Q3, Measuring principle, Accuracy class, MPEs, Temperature class, Water pressure class, Pressure loss class, Reverse flow, Environmental class, Electromagnetic environment, Mechanical class, Maximum admissible temperature.\n\nPage 5 first table: Maximum admissible pressure 1.6 MPa, Orientation limitation any, Indicating range: colspan2 \"99 999\", \"99 999\", \"99 999\", empty; Resolution 0.000 1 ×3; rapid testing resolution \"-\"; EUT testing requirements: Category, Case D; Installation details: Flanged, 0, 0, U0D0, No, -, any, -; Length: colspan2 200, 200, 200, empty; electrical installation details; Power supply note.\n\nSo page 4 + page 5 first table form the DN25/32/40 block. So DN25/32/40: indicating range 99 999, resolution 0.000 1, length 200 each.\n\nPage 5 second table: Manufacturer, Model number, DN 50/65/80, Q1: 0.252/0.158, 0.400/0.250, 0.640/0.400; Q2: 0.403/0.252, 0.640/0.400, 1.024/0.640; Q3: 63.0, 100, 160; Q4: 73.0, 125, 200; Q3/Q1: 250/400 each.\n\nPage 6 first table: Measuring principle, Accuracy class, MPEs, Temperature class, Water pressure class MAP16, Pressure loss Δp40, Reverse flow, Environmental class, Electromagnetic environment, Mechanical class, Max admissible temperature, Max admissible pressure 1.6, Orientation limitation any, Indicating range: colspan2 \"99 999\", colspan2 \"999 999\", colspan2 \"999 999\"; Resolution: \"0.000 1\", \"0.001\", \"0.001\"; rapid testing \"-\"; EUT: Category, Case D; Installation details: Flanged, 0, 0, U0D0, No, -, any, -; Length: 200, 200, 200; electrical; power supply note.\n\nSo DN50: indicating range 99 999, resolution 0.000 1, length 200.\nDN65: indicating range 999 999, resolution 0.001, length 200.\nDN80: indicating range 999 999, resolution 0.001, length 200.\n\nPage 7 first table: Orientation limitations \"-\", Power supply note. (continuation)\n\nPage 7 second table: DN 100/125/150: Q1: 1.00/0.63, 1.60/1.00, 2.52/1.60; Q2: 1.60/1.00, 2.56/1.60, 4.03/1.56; Q3: 250, 400, 630; Q4: 313, 500, 780; Q3/Q1: 250/400; ... Measuring principle, Accuracy class, MPEs, Temperature class, Water pressure class, Pressure loss, Reverse flow, Environmental class, EM environment, Mechanical class, Max temp, Max pressure 1.6, Orientation limitation any, Indicating range: 999 999 ×3, Resolution: 0.001 ×3, rapid \"-\", EUT category.\n\nPage 8 first table: Case D, Installation details: Flanged, 0, 0, U0D0, No, -, any, -; Length: 250, 250, 300; electrical; power supply.\n\nSo DN100: indicating 999 999, resolution 0.001, length 250.\nDN125: indicating 999 999, resolution 0.001, length 250.\nDN150: indicating 999 999, resolution 0.001, length 300.\n\nPage 8 second table: DN 200/250/300: Q1: 4.0/2.5, 6.4/4.0, 6.4/4.0; Q2: 6.4/4.0, 10.2/6.4, 10.2/6.4; Q3: 1000, 1600, 1600; Q4: 1250, 2000, 2000; Q3/Q1 250/400; Measuring principle, Accuracy class, MPEs, Temperature class.\n\nPage 9 first table: Water pressure class MAP16, Pressure loss Δp40, Reverse flow, Environmental class, EM env, Mechanical class, Max temp, Max pressure 1.6, Orientation limitation any, Indicating range: 9 999 999 ×3, Resolution: 0.01 ×3, rapid \"-\", EUT: Category, Case D, Installation details: Flanged, 0, 0, U0D0, No, -, any, -; Length: 350, 450, 500; electrical; power supply.\n\nSo DN200: indicating 9 999 999, resolution 0.01, length 350.\nDN250: indicating 9 999 999, resolution 0.01, length 450.\nDN300: indicating 9 999 999, resolution 0.01, length 500.\n\nPage 9-10: DN350 table: Q1: 6.4/4.0; Q2: 10.2/6.4; Q3: 1600; Q4: 2000; Q3/Q1: 250/400; ... Indicating range 9 999 999, Resolution 0.01, ... Length 550.\n\nSo DN350: indicating 9 999 999, resolution 0.01, length 550.\n\nPower supply and electrical specification table:\nMV110:\n- MS2500 / MV110 HV: 100–240 V AC, 45–66 Hz, internal backup 3.7V DC 5200mAh Battery\n- MS2500 / MV110 LV: 24–36 V AC/DC, 45–66 Hz, internal backup 3.7V DC 5200mAh Battery\n- MS2500 / MV110 LLV: 10–48 V DC, no frequency, no backup\n- Software version: V.1.04.0005.0004 (for MV110)\n- CRC: 9BA34F3B (for MV110)\n- Cut-off: 0.2%\n- Detection empty pipe: on\n\nMV145:\n- MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery rec.: 100–240 V AC, 45–66 Hz, backup 3.7V DC 5200mAh\n- MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery: 10–48 V DC, (frequency blank), backup 9V DC\n- MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery Li-SOCl2: 10–48 V DC, no freq, backup 3.6V DC\n- Software version: V.1.05.0004.0005 for MV145\n- CRC: C96453B1 for MV145\n- Cut-off: 0.2%\n- Detection empty pipe: on\n\nModel family structure. The models could be modeled as DN variants with two accuracy/ratio classes. That's 13 DNs × 2 = 26 variants. Plus 6 module power supply variants. That's a lot.\n\nHow should I structure this? The schema says model_family should include models when cert covers >1 model variant. I think the cleanest approach:\n\nmodel_family.family_name: null (or \"MS2500\"?) The sensor family is MS2500. Certified types are MS2500/MV110 and MS2500/MV145.\n\nmodels[]: I could create model_id like \"DN25_Q3/Q1=250\" and \"DN25_Q3/Q1=400\"... 26 entries. Or model_id per DN: \"DN25\", \"DN32\", ..., \"DN350\" (13 entries), with attributes including both class-1 and class-2 values via config_level (axis: accuracy_class/Q3-Q1 ratio).\n\nActually the Q3/Q1 ratio axis: for each DN, two columns: Q3/Q1=250 (class 1) and Q3/Q1=400 (class 2). So the config axis is \"Q3/Q1 ratio\" with conditions 250 and 400.\n\nI think a good approach:\n- model_family.models: 13 entries, model_id \"DN25\" ... \"DN350\", each with attributes {converter: [\"MV110\",\"MV145\"], ...}.\n- model_level characteristics: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1 — but q1/q2/q3/q4 vary by both model AND the Q3/Q1 ratio config. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, make models = DN + ratio combos: \"DN25 Q3/Q1=250\", etc. That makes model_level simpler: q1, q2, q3, q4 values per model directly. And accuracy_class: 1 for 250-ratio, 2 for 400-ratio.\n\nGiven the guidance \"Lists of values... is a MODEL MATRIX — emit model_level entries\", I think using 26 model variants (DN × ratio) is cleanest for the matrix. Let me do that.\n\nmodels:\n- model_id: \"MS2500 DN25 Q3/Q1=250 (class 1)\" — maybe simpler: \"DN25-250\". Hmm. Let me use model_id like \"DN25/Q3=16 m³/h\"? No.\n\nLet me use: model_id: \"DN25 (Q3/Q1=250)\" and \"DN25 (Q3/Q1=400)\". With label attributes: {\"nominal_dn_mm\": 25, \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"q3_ratio\": 250}.\n\nActually attributes{} can hold: {\"converter\": [\"MV110\",\"MV145\"], \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 25, \"accuracy_class\": 1}.\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- minimum_flowrate_q1: values per model (unit m³/h)\n- transitional_flowrate_q2\n- permanent_flowrate_q3\n- overload_flowrate_q4\n- ratio_q3_over_q1: 250 or 400\n\nType-level characteristics (common):\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- measuring_principle: Electromagnetic water meter\n- mpe_lower_zone: per class — this varies by accuracy class, which is a config axis. Could do config_level: attribute maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone, axis accuracy_class, values: [{condition: \"class 1\", value: -3..3?}, ...]. MPE ±3% → value could be {min: -3, max: 3}? Or string \"±3%\". Hmm. Rule: ranges become {min,max}. ±3% is symmetric. I could record value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\" with the ± implied... Better to keep faithful: value: {min: -3, max: 3}, unit_symbol: \"%\". Hmm, that might be overinterpretation. Alternatively value: \"±3\". Let me think — the rule says numbers not strings where possible. I'll use {min: -3, max: 3} with unit \"%\" and include a note? There's no note field for type_level... The structured value shape is {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. I'll go with {min:-3,max:3}.\n\nActually, for MPE_u it's temperature-dependent: ±1 (class1) ±2 (class2) % for ≤30°C; ±2 (class1) ±3 (class2) % for >30°C. This is config_level with axis \"water_temperature and accuracy_class\". Conditions: {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"water_temp\": \"≤30°C\"} → ±1%... I'll encode:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone, axis: \"water_temperature_and_accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"class 1, water ≤ 30 °C\", value: {min:-1,max:1}, unit: \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 2, water ≤ 30 °C\", value: {min:-2,max:2}, unit:\"%\"}, {condition: \"class 1, water > 30 °C\", value: {min:-2,max:2}}, {condition: \"class 2, water > 30 °C\", value: {min:-3,max:3}}]\n- attribute: maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone, axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"class 1\", value: {min:-3,max:3}, unit: \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 2\", value: {min:-5,max:5}, unit: \"%\"}]\n\n- temperature_class: varies by liner → config_level: attribute temperature_class, axis liner_material, values: [{condition: \"PTFE\", value: \"T90; T50; T30\"}, {condition: \"Rilsan\", value: \"T50; T30 (from DN50)\"}, {condition: \"Ebonite\", value: \"T50; T30 (from DN200)\"}, {condition: \"polypropylene\", value: \"T50; T30\"}]\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: config by liner: PTFE 90, others 50, unit °C.\n- water_pressure_class: MAP16 → type_level: value \"MAP16\"\n- pressure_loss_class: Δp40 → type_level \"Δp40\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: value \"O\", with range -25 to +55 °C. I'll do environmental_class: \"O (-25 to +55 °C)\" as string? Or split: environmental_class: \"O\" and operating_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55, \"°C\"}. I'll do both: environmental_class: \"O\"; operating_temperature_range: {min:-25,max:55} °C.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\" — list. Could be string \"E1, E2\".\n- mechanical_class: \"B, O\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range: varies by DN → model_level: unit m³: DN25: 99999, DN32: 99999, DN40: 99999, DN50: 99999, DN65: 999999, DN80: 999999, DN100: 999999, DN125: 999999, DN150: 999999, DN200: 9999999, DN250: 9999999, DN300: 9999999, DN350: 9999999.\n- resolution_of_indicating_device: model_level: 0.0001 for DN25-50; 0.001 for DN65-150; 0.01 for DN200-350.\n- length: model_level: 200,200,200,200,200,200,250,250,300,350,450,500,550 mm.\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- min straight inlet/outlet: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_sensitivity_class: U0 D0 (marking says Ux Dx doesn't differ from U0 D0)\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- eut_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- eut_case: \"D\"\n- volume_resolution_display: 0.001 m³ (from description: \"displays the volume resolution of 0.001 m3 on the digital display\") — hmm, this conflicts slightly with resolution table, but it's in the description. I could include display_volume_resolution: 0.001 m³. It's from the description text. Include it? It's a characteristic mentioned. I'll include it as type_level since stated generally.\n\nPower supply — this varies by module designation (6 modules). Use config_level with axis \"module_designation\":\n- power_supply_voltage: HV: {100-240} V (AC); LV: {24-36} V AC/DC; LLV: {10-48} V DC; MV145 HV: {100-240} V AC; MV145 LLV + alk: {10-48} V DC; MV145 LLV + lit: {10-48} V DC.\n- power_supply_type: AC / AC/DC / DC...\n- power_supply_frequency: 45-66 Hz for HV/LV (both MV110 and MV145 HV); null for DC variants.\n- internal_backup_source_voltage: 3.7 V (MV110 HV, MV110 LV, MV145 HV), 9 V (MV145 LLV + alk), 3.6 V (MV145 LLV + lit Li-SOCl2), none for MV110 LLV.\n- backup capacity: 5200 mAh where stated.\n\nSoftware: \n- software_version: config by electronics: MV110: \"V.1.04.0005.0004\"; MV145: \"V.1.05.0004.0005\"\n- crc_checksum: MV110: \"9BA34F3B\"; MV145: \"C96453B1\"\n- cut_off: 0.2 %\n- empty_pipe_detection: \"on\"\n\nComponents: The system consists of sensor MS2500 and converter MV110 or MV145. So:\ncomponents:\n- role: sensor, type_designations: [\"MS2500\"], characteristics: liner options etc.\n- role: converter/electronic calculator-indicator, type_designations: [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"], alternatives: \"OR\"\n\nLiner options could be part of sensor component characteristics. I'll keep it simpler — the liner detail is captured in config_level for temperature class.\n\nModule designation field: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", etc. The schema's module_designation is a single string, but here there are 6. I could list them in the certified_type.type_designations or module_designation. The schema shows module_designation as string (\"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null). Since there are multiple, maybe put the full list... Hmm. I think I can put the 6 module designations as a combined string or put them in components. Actually, these module designations are really full model+module combos. I'll set module_designation to null and include the variants in model_family? No — better: since the field exists and cert explicitly has \"Designation of the module\" section, I'll include module_designation as a string listing... but schema expects single. Alternative: include modules as a components entry? The \"module\" here for R49 water meters is the meter itself (water meters are typically the whole instrument). Actually for R49, the module concept: the water meter consists of sensor + converter (measuring transducer + electronic calculator + indicator). The \"Designation of the module\" listing HV/LV/LLV variants are power-supply modifications of the complete instrument.\n\nI'll represent these 6 as module designations in the certified_type via a separate approach: I'll put module_designation as a string with all six joined by \"; \". That's pragmatic. Or omit. Hmm, rule 8: don't fabricate; field present in cert though. I'll include as a single string: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV; MS2500 / MV110 LV; MS2500 / MV110 LLV; MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\". That preserves data.\n\nActually, maybe cleaner: use config_level power supply axis conditions keyed by these module designations. Yes, I planned that.\n\nNow decide model IDs. 26 combos might be verbose but thorough. Let me define models as DN-based (13) and use config axis \"Q3/Q1 ratio\" for q1/q2/q4 differences? But then q3 is constant per DN. q1 differs by ratio: e.g., DN25: Q1=0.064 (ratio 250) vs 0.040 (ratio 400). So q1, q2, q3/Q1 vary by ratio; q3, q4 constant per DN.\n\nOption A: models = 13 DNs; config_level axis \"q3_over_q1_ratio\" for q1 and q2.\nOption B: models = 26 DN+ratio combos; model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4.\n\nOption B is more explicit and matches the table layout (each DN has two columns: class 1 and class 2). The accuracy class column confirms: for each DN, columns are [class1 (ratio 250), class2 (ratio 400)]. I'll go with Option B: 26 models.\n\nmodel_id naming: \"DN25-Q3/Q1=250\" maybe. Let me use \"DN25 (Q3/Q1=250)\" — parentheses fine in JSON. Or simpler \"DN25-250\". I'll use \"DN25_Q3Q1_250\"? Hmm readability. I'll go with \"DN25 (Q3/Q1=250)\".\n\nAttributes per model: {\"nominal_diameter_mm\": 25, \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"converters\": [\"MV110\",\"MV145\"]}. Keep attributes simple: nominal_diameter_mm, accuracy_class.\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n1. q1 (minimum flowrate) — 26 values\n2. q2 (transitional flowrate) — 26 values\n3. q3 (permanent flowrate) — 26 values (pairs equal)\n4. q4 (overload flowrate) — 26 values (pairs equal)\n5. q3_over_q1 ratio — 26 values (250/400) — or derive from model attributes. Include for completeness? It's redundant with attributes. I'll include ratio_q3_over_q1 in model attributes only... Actually simpler to include as model_level attribute? It's part of model identity. I'll put in attributes: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\": 250. Then skip model_level entry for it.\n6. indicating_range — per DN (13 distinct values, but repeated across the 2 variants). For model_level with 26 models, each model gets its DN's value. That's fine.\n7. resolution_of_indicating_device — same.\n8. length — same.\n\nThat's a lot of JSON but correct.\n\nLet me compile the data table:\n\nDN25: ratio250: Q1=0.064, Q2=0.102; ratio400: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064; Q3=16.00, Q4=20.00; class 1/2; ind=99999; res=0.0001; len=200\nDN32: ratio250: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160; ratio400: Q1=0.062, Q2=0.100; Q3=25.00, Q4=31.25; ind=99999; res=0.0001; len=200\nDN40: ratio250: Q1=0.160, Q2=0.256; ratio400: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160; Q3=40.00, Q4=50.00; ind=99999; res=0.0001; len=200\nDN50: ratio250: Q1=0.252, Q2=0.403; ratio400: Q1=0.158, Q2=0.252; Q3=63.0, Q4=73.0; ind=99999; res=0.0001; len=200\nDN65: ratio250: Q1=0.400, Q2=0.640; ratio400: Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400; Q3=100, Q4=125; ind=999999; res=0.001; len=200\nDN80: ratio250: Q1=0.640, Q2=1.024; ratio400: Q1=0.400, Q2=0.640; Q3=160, Q4=200; ind=999999; res=0.001; len=200\nDN100: ratio250: Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60; ratio400: Q1=0.63, Q2=1.00; Q3=250, Q4=313; ind=999999; res=0.001; len=250\nDN125: ratio250: Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56; ratio400: Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60; Q3=400, Q4=500; ind=999999; res=0.001; len=250\nDN150: ratio250: Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03; ratio400: Q1=1.60, Q2=1.56; Q3=630, Q4=780; ind=999999; res=0.001; len=300\nDN200: ratio250: Q1=4.0, Q2=6.4; ratio400: Q1=2.5, Q2=4.0; Q3=1000, Q4=1250; ind=9999999; res=0.01; len=350\nDN250: ratio250: Q1=6.4, Q2=10.2; ratio400: Q1=4.0, Q2=6.4; Q3=1600, Q4=2000; ind=9999999; res=0.01; len=450\nDN300: ratio250: Q1=6.4, Q2=10.2; ratio400: Q1=4.0, Q2=6.4; Q3=1600, Q4=2000; ind=9999999; res=0.01; len=500\nDN350: ratio250: Q1=6.4, Q2=10.2; ratio400: Q1=4.0, Q2=6.4; Q3=1600, Q4=2000; ind=9999999; res=0.01; len=550\n\nAlso matrix_tables: I should preserve the DN flow tables as a matrix table. Combine into one matrix table \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter\" with columns: nominal_diameter, accuracy_class, q3_over_q1, q1_m3h, q2_m3h, q3_m3h, q4_m3h, indicating_range_m3, resolution_m3, length_mm. Rows = 26. That's a good matrix_tables entry. And a second matrix table for power supply.\n\nPower supply matrix table:\ncolumns: electronics, module_designation, supply_type, voltage, frequency_hz, internal_backup_source.\nRows:\nMV110 | MS2500 / MV110 HV | mains AC | 100–240 V | 45–66 | 3.7 V DC 5200 mAh battery\nMV110 | MS2500 / MV110 LV | mains AC/DC | 24–36 V | 45–66 | 3.7 V DC 5200 mAh battery\nMV110 | MS2500 / MV110 LLV | mains DC | 10–48 V | null | none\nMV145 | MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery | mains AC | 100–240 V | 45–66 | 3.7 V DC 5200 mAh\nMV145 | MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery | mains DC | 10–48 V | null | 9 V DC alkaline battery\nMV145 | MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery (Li-SOCl2) | mains DC | 10–48 V | null | 3.6 V DC lithium battery (Li-SOCl2)\n\nNote: on page 1, the module list says \"MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery\" — page 1 doesn't list MV145 HV... wait: page 1 module designations: MS2500 / MV110 HV, LV, LLV; MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery, MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery, MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery. Yes 6 total. Good.\n\nPower supply config_level entries with axis \"module_designation\":\n- power_supply_voltage: conditions \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\" → {min:100,max:240} V; \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\" → {min:24,max:36} V; \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\" → {min:10,max:48} V; \"MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery\" → {100,240} V; \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery\" → {10,48} V; \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\" → {10,48} V.\n- power_supply_type: \"AC mains\", \"AC/DC mains\", \"DC mains\"... The table column is \"Type (battery, mains AC, mains DC):\" values: \"100 – 240 V AC\", \"24 – 36V AC/DC\", \"10 – 48 V DC\". So power_supply_type: AC / AC+DC / DC. I'll encode: \"AC\" / \"AC/DC\" / \"DC\".\n- power_supply_frequency: {45,66} Hz for HV/LV and MV145 HV; absent/null for DC-only. Only include rows with values? For LLV it's \"-\". Include condition with value null? I'll omit null conditions.\n- internal_backup_source: \"3.7 V DC, 5200 mAh lithium battery\" etc. For LLV: \"-\" (none). I'll encode as strings since mixed. Or split into voltage and capacity: internal_backup_voltage: 3.7 V; 9 V; 3.6 V. And backup_battery_capacity: 5200 mAh where stated. Hmm. Keep it as strings: value: \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh; lithium battery\". Strings allowed when not purely numeric. I'll do structured where clean:\n  - internal_backup_source_voltage: 3.7 V (MV110 HV, MV110 LV, MV145 HV+lit), 9 V (MV145 LLV+alk), 3.6 V (MV145 LLV+lit Li-SOCl2)\n  Maybe simpler one attribute with string values. I'll do:\n  config_level: attribute \"internal_backup_source\", axis \"module_designation\", values with strings: \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh (Li battery)\", \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh (Li battery)\", null (MV110 LLV), \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh\", \"9 V DC (alkaline battery)\", \"3.6 V DC (Li-SOCl2 lithium battery)\". Omit the null one or include value null. I'll omit MV110 LLV since \"-\".\n\nSoftware config_level:\n- software_version, axis \"converter_electronics\": MV110 → \"V.1.04.0005.0004\"; MV145 → \"V.1.05.0004.0005\"\n- crc_checksum: MV110 → \"9BA34F3B\"; MV145 → \"C96453B1\"\n- cut_off: 0.2 % (type_level)\n- empty_pipe_detection: \"on\" (type_level)\n\nComponents:\n1. role: \"sensor (measurement transducer)\", type_designations: [\"MS2500\"], characteristics: body steel with flanges, excitation coils, electrodes; liner options: PTFE (DN25–DN350, up to 90°C), polypropylene (DN25–DN150, up to 50°C), Rilsan (DN50–DN350, up to 50°C), Ebonite (DN200–DN350, up to 50°C). Could put liner options into characteristics{} as a nested object. characteristics is free-form object. I'll add: {\"liner_options\": [{\"material\": \"PTFE\", \"dn_range\": {...}, \"max_water_temperature\": ...}, ...]}. That's reasonable.\n2. role: \"converter (electronic calculator and indicator)\", type_designations: [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {display: \"LCD\", interfaces: [...]}.\n\nKeep components modest.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\". The description says \"Measuring system description\" heading but content is water meters. R49 → water meters. I'll use \"Water meter\".\n\ncertified_type.type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV145\"]. Also model numbers \"MS2500-P80-B1A2B\" from label? That's an example nameplate (Mod MS2500-P80-B1A2B, MV110-B1B4B