licants: [{name: \"ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA\", address_lines: [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo\", \"20 099 Italy\"]}]\nmanufacturers: [{name: \"Hemina SPA\", address_lines: [\"Via Piemonte 1\", \"Montagnana – PD\", \"PC: 350 44\", \"Italy\"]}]\n\nThe characteristics tables say Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy. Address conflict (page 1: Via Piemonte 1; tables: Via Piemonte 2). Use page 1 for manufacturers. Fine.\n\ndate_issued: The certificate Revision 2 date: 2 February 2023. \"Date: 2 February 2023\". Good.\n\ntest_reports:\n1. {id: \"0511-ER-V053-22\", date: \"2022-08-10\", pages: 381, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n2. {id: \"6015-PT-P5009-22\", pages: 21, role: \"test_report\"}\n3. {id: \"8551-PT-E0072-22\", pages: 15, role: \"test_report\"}\n4. {id: \"6011-PT-SW012-22\", pages: 6, role: \"test_report\"}\n5. {id: \"6015-PT-P5001-22 Revision 1\", pages: 104, role: \"test_report\"} — id \"6015-PT-P5001-22\" with revision 1? The id field is string; I'll use \"6015-PT-P5001-22 (Revision 1)\".\n6. {id: \"6015-PT-P5001-22\", pages: 94, role: \"test_report\"}\n7. {id: \"6011-PT-SW009-22\", pages: 9, role: \"test_report\"}\n8. {id: \"8551-PT-E0041-21\", pages: 29, role: \"test_report\"}\n\ndocumentation file: test_reports role \"documentation_file\": {id: \"0511-UL-V053-20\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nrevision_history: as above with revision strings \"0\",\"1\",\"2\".\n\nfootnotes: none in the doc. Omit.\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [1, 2]}. Edition as int 2013. Hmm schema says edition (int). Yes.\n\nNow MPE encoding. Let me reconsider: ±3% → value {min: -3, max: 3}, unit \"%\". I think that's reasonable and structured. Alternatively keep as string \"±3\". I'll use {min:-3,max:3}.\n\nFor the temperature-dependent MPE_u, config_level:\n\n{\n \"attribute\": \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\",\n \"axis\": \"water_temperature_and_accuracy_class\",\n \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"accuracy class 1, water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -1, \"max\": 1}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"accuracy class 2, water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"accuracy class 1, water temperature > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"accuracy class 2, water temperature > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n ]\n}\n\nConfig value shape: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\" per rule 5.\n\nTemperature class config:\n{\n \"attribute\": \"temperature_class\",\n \"axis\": \"liner_material\",\n \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"PTFE liner\", \"value\": \"T90, T50, T30\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Rilsan liner\", \"value\": \"T50, T30 (from DN50)\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Ebonite liner\", \"value\": \"T50, T30 (from DN200)\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"polypropylene liner\", \"value\": \"T50, T30\"}\n ]\n}\n\nMax admissible temperature config:\n{\n \"attribute\": \"maximum_admissible_water_temperature\",\n \"axis\": \"liner_material\",\n \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"PTFE liner\", \"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"polypropylene liner\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Rilsan liner\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"Ebonite liner\", \"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n ]\n}\n\nSensor liner DN ranges (from description):\n- PTFE: DN25–DN350, 90°C\n- Polypropylene: DN25–DN150, 50°C\n- Rilsan: DN50–DN350, 50°C\n- Ebonite: DN200–DN350, 50°C\n\nPut in components sensor characteristics.\n\nType-level characteristics list:\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\"\n- operating_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\"\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- mounting_orientation: \"any\" — redundant; keep orientation: any once.\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- minimum_straight_inlet_pipe_length: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_outlet_pipe_length: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: false / \"No\"\n- eut_test_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- eut_test_case: \"D\"\n- cut_off: 0.2 \"%\"\n- empty_pipe_detection: \"on\"\n- display_volume_resolution: 0.001 m³ (from description)\n\nFor type_level shape: {label: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}}. Values as per rules.\n\nNow model IDs. I'll use format \"DN25 (Q3/Q1=250)\". Alternatively include converter? Both converters share flow specs. Keep attributes: {\"nominal_diameter_mm\": 25, \"q3_over_q1\": 250, \"accuracy_class\": 1}.\n\nModel list (26):\nDN25 (Q3/Q1=250), DN25 (Q3/Q1=400), DN32 ×2, DN40 ×2, DN50 ×2, DN65 ×2, DN80 ×2, DN100 ×2, DN125 ×2, DN150 ×2, DN200 ×2, DN250 ×2, DN300 ×2, DN350 ×2.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- q1 (unit m³/h): 26 values\n- q2: 26 values\n- q3: 26 values\n- q4: 26 values\n- indicating_range (m³): 26 values\n- resolution_of_indicating_device (m³): 26 values\n- length (mm): 26 values\n\nThat's 7 × 26 = 182 value objects. Large but fine.\n\nHmm, is this too heavy? The instruction emphasizes matrix detection and preserving data. I'll do it. Alternatively 13 models with config axis ratio for q1/q2. The accuracy class column literally pairs with ratio columns, so 26 models is faithful. Go with 26.\n\nWait — also Q2/Q1 and Q4/Q3 constant → type_level. Accuracy class 1 ↔ ratio 250; class 2 ↔ ratio 400. Include accuracy_class in model attributes.\n\nMatrix table: one combined table with 26 rows and columns: nominal_diameter, accuracy_class, q3_over_q1, q1_m3h, q2_